1. Wayu-Paxa-TTS-Edge (TTH engine "wayu", optional, runs locally)
   Model weights: https://huggingface.co/wayu-ai/wayu-paxa-tts-edge
   By Paxa Labs / Wayu Research (Kunat Pipatanakul, Potsawee Manakul, Warit Sirichotedumrong,
   Sittipong Sripaisarnmongkol, Pakorn Nathong, Phatrasek Jirabovonvisut).
   License of the weights: CC-BY-NC-4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/).
   Attribution is required and commercial use is NOT permitted. Using or downloading the weights also means
   agreeing to the Wayu Research Acceptable Use terms (https://www.wayuresearch.org/terms#acceptable-use).
   The weights are fetched from Hugging Face by the wayu_tts package on first use and are never stored in or
   shipped with this repository. Audio made with the "wayu" engine is therefore for non-commercial use only.
   The voices are synthetic: do not present the audio as a recording of a real person, and say that it is
   machine-generated wherever a listener could be misled.
   Paper: "Building and Evaluating Fixed-Voice Thai TTS from Synthetic Speech", arXiv:2609.03502 (2026).

   Inference code: wayu-tts, https://github.com/wayu-research/wayu-tts-inference
   (commit 07ab898d516649313926f0b843b809a962f993ad), Apache-2.0. Installed by the "tth" extra.
   It builds on Kokoro (https://github.com/hexgrad/kokoro, Apache-2.0), tltk and PyThaiNLP.

2. edge-tts (TTH engine "edge", optional fallback, online)
   https://github.com/rany2/edge-tts, LGPL-3.0, installed by the "tth" or "tth-edge" extra.
   It sends the text to the Microsoft Edge read-aloud service over the internet to make the audio; the service
   and its voices (for example th-TH-PremwadeeNeural) belong to Microsoft and follow Microsoft's terms.

3. Web demo speech (docs/index.html, "Speak replies (TTH)")
   Uses the browser's Web Speech API with a Thai voice already installed on the device. TER-AI ships no voice
   for it and sends nothing; how the device or browser makes the speech is up to its maker.

TTH does not use the Paxa Labs cloud API (Paxa TTS Flash).

SANKPUP (pictures from code, v0.11.0)
SANKPUP uses no third-party model or data inside this repository. The optional parts below are installed or
downloaded separately and keep their own licenses.

4. Gemma 4 (SANKPUP engine "gemma", optional, runs locally)
   Model weights by Google DeepMind: official QAT Q4_0 GGUF builds, e.g.
   https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-E2B-it-qat-q4_0-gguf
   License of the weights: Apache License 2.0 (https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/gemma_4_license).
   Downloaded on first use into ~/.cache/terai/models and checked against the published SHA-256.

5. llama-cpp-python (https://github.com/abetlen/llama-cpp-python), MIT. Installed by the "sankpup-gemma" extra.

6. resvg-py (https://github.com/baseplate-admin/resvg-py), MIT, wrapping resvg
   (https://github.com/linebender/resvg), Apache-2.0; Pillow (https://python-pillow.org), MIT-CMU.
   Installed by the "sankpup" and "sankpup-gemma" extras.

SANKPUP does not call any cloud API.

MuML 1.0 WA (music in the browser with the Web Audio API, v0.12.0)
Unlike TTH and SANKPUP, MuML 1.0 WA includes third-party-derived data in this repository:

7. Nottingham Music Database, as cleaned by jukedeck/nottingham-dataset
   Repository: https://github.com/jukedeck/nottingham-dataset (commit 0992bb6cd864f6d4b90d6663e08c0ef53ffaa08f)
   Original ABC files: http://abc.sourceforge.net/NMD/
   License: GNU General Public License v3.0 (the repository's LICENSE.md).
   Used for: the note table src/terai_router/muml_wa/data/markov.json and its web copy docs/muml_wa_table.js
   (counts of pitch, chord and rhythm transitions; no tune is stored). These two files are GPL-3.0; the full
   license text is next to each of them (LICENSE-GPL-3.0.txt). The code that reads them stays MIT.

The note writer in v0.12.0 is a seeded Markov chain ("markov-v1"), not the trained MuML 1.0 network. All sound is
synthesized by the Web Audio API in the browser: no soundfont, no samples, no server, no cloud API.

MUML 1.0 HIQ (text-to-music audio, prototype, not released)
MUML 1.0 HIQ ships only TER-AI code (MIT) in this repository: the Thai/English prompt adapter and the
wrapper. The model and runtime below are downloaded or built separately and keep their own licenses.

8. ACE-Step 1.5 (turbo DiT "acestep-v15-turbo", VAE) by ACE Studio and StepFun
   https://huggingface.co/ACE-Step/Ace-Step1.5 , https://github.com/ace-step/ACE-Step-1.5
   License: MIT (code and weights). Not gated on Hugging Face.
   GGUF conversions used at run time: https://huggingface.co/Serveurperso/ACE-Step-1.5-GGUF (MIT).
   Downloaded on first use into ~/.cache/terai/muml-hiq/models (or $TERAI_MUML_HIQ_MODELS).

9. Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B (ACE-Step 1.5 text encoder) by the Qwen team, Apache License 2.0
   https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B (GGUF Q8_0 from the repository in 8).

10. acestep.cpp by ServeurpersoCom, MIT, built on ggml (MIT)
   https://github.com/ServeurpersoCom/acestep.cpp , pinned commit de858d8e1a94f0e1939cf9bdd8ff7d9631953077.
   Cloned and built by `terai muml --hiq --setup`.

MUML 1.0 HIQ does not fine-tune or redistribute these weights and does not call any cloud API.
